name: cutting text apart
boss: Sieve
flavor: What passes through me is all that remains.
brief: cut takes columns out of a line: -f fields with -d as the separator, or
brief: -c characters by position. tr rewrites or deletes characters, one for
brief: one, and only ever reads standard input -- it takes no filename.
brief:
brief: cut's default delimiter is TAB, not space. That surprises everyone once.
brief: For anything space-separated, awk is the better tool, and it is next.
---
q: %show% the first comma-separated field of every line in @csv@
a: cut -d, -f1 @csv@
out: ^gateway$
ref: cut
teach: cut -d
teach: cut -f
hint: -d sets the delimiter, -f picks the field number
---
q: %show% the third comma-separated field of @csv@
a: cut -d, -f3 @csv@
out: ^up$
ref: cut
teach: cut -f
hint: field numbers start at 1, not 0
---
q: %show% fields 1 and 3 of @csv@
a: cut -d, -f1,3 @csv@
out: gateway,up
ref: cut
teach: cut -f
hint: comma-separate the field numbers to pick more than one
---
q: %show% fields 2 through 4 of @csv@
a: cut -d, -f2-4 @csv@
out: 1,up,42
ref: cut
teach: cut -f
hint: a dash between two numbers means a range
---
q: %show% everything from field 3 to the end of each line in @csv@
a: cut -d, -f3- @csv@
out: up,42
ref: cut
teach: cut -f
hint: a trailing dash with no end number means to the end of the line
---
q: %show% the first field of @csv@ treating any run of whitespace as the delimiter
a: cut -D -f1 @csv@
ref: cut
teach: cut -D
hint: capital D treats consecutive whitespace as one delimiter
---
q: %show% the first 10 characters of each line of @log@
a: cut -c1-10 @log@
out: ^2024-03-01$
ref: cut
teach: cut -c
hint: -c counts characters by position, not fields
---
q: %show% characters 12 onward of each line of @log@
a: cut -c12- @log@
out: [0-9]
ref: cut
teach: cut -c
hint: same trailing-dash trick as field ranges, but for characters
---
q: %show% the first 8 bytes of each line of @log@
a: cut -b1-8 @log@
out: ^2024-03
ref: cut
teach: cut -b
hint: -b counts bytes; same as -c unless the text is multibyte
---
q: %show% field 1 of @csv@, skipping lines that have no comma in them at all
a: cut -d, -f1 -s @csv@
out: ^gateway$
ref: cut
teach: cut -s
hint: without -s, a line with no delimiter is printed whole
---
q: %show% field 1 of @csv@ separating the output with a colon instead of a comma
a: cut -d, -f1,3 -O: @csv@
ref: cut
teach: cut -O
hint: -O sets the OUTPUT delimiter, separate from -d which sets the input one
---
q: {uppercase everything in @txt@|%turn% @txt@ to upper case|make @txt@ all capitals}
a: tr a-z A-Z < @txt@
out: ROTATE THE LOGS
ref: tr
teach: tr
hint: tr has no filename argument -- redirect the file into it
---
q: {lowercase everything in @txt@|%turn% @txt@ to lower case|make @txt@ all small letters}
a: tr A-Z a-z < @txt@
out: rotate
ref: tr
teach: tr
hint: same idea as uppercasing, just with the ranges swapped
---
q: {delete every digit from @log@|strip all the digits out of @log@|remove every numeral from @log@}
a: tr -d 0-9 < @log@
out: INFO
ref: tr
teach: tr -d
hint: d deletes every character in the set, with nothing to replace it
---
q: {strip the newlines out of @txt@ so it becomes one long line|delete every newline from @txt@, collapsing it to one line|remove the line breaks from @txt@ so it reads as a single line}
a: tr -d '\n' < @txt@
out: rotate
ref: tr
teach: tr -d
hint: \n is the newline character, deleted the same as any other
---
q: {collapse every run of repeated spaces in @log@ down to a single space|squeeze repeated spaces in @log@ down to one space each|collapse runs of spaces in @log@ so each run becomes a single space}
a: tr -s ' ' < @log@
out: INFO service
ref: tr
teach: tr -s
hint: s for squeeze
---
q: %turn% every space in @txt@ into a newline, one word per line
a: tr ' ' '\n' < @txt@
out: ^rotate$
ref: tr
teach: tr
hint: tr maps the first set onto the second, one character at a time
---
q: {delete everything from @txt@ except letters and newlines|keep only letters and newlines in @txt@, deleting the rest|strip @txt@ down to just letters and newlines}
a: tr -dc 'a-zA-Z\n' < @txt@
out: rotate
ref: tr
teach: tr -c
hint: -c complements the set, so -dc deletes everything NOT listed

---
q: {sort the numbers in the count column of @csv@ numerically, largest first|order the count column of @csv@ numerically, biggest number first|pull the count column from @csv@ and sort it numerically, descending}
a: cut -d, -f4 @csv@ | sort -rn
out: 42
ref: sort
teach: sort -n
hint: without -n, 7 sorts after 42 because it compares text
